22:04:17 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That’s a very common and understandable assumption—but a thermostat doesn’t actually make your whole house the exact same temperature everywhere. It’s more like a single-point sample that controls when the heating system turns on and off, rather than a uniform climate-control field. Here’s what really happens: ### 1. A thermostat measures only one spot A thermostat contains a temperature sensor right where it’s mounted—usually on an interior wall at about chest height. It has no idea what the temperature is in the corner of the room, near a window, on the floor, or in a different room. When that one spot reaches 72°F, the heat shuts off. The rest of the house can be significantly different, especially in winter. ### 2. Temperature varies throughout a room In winter, cold outside walls and windows create a gradient: near an exterior wall it could be 66°F while the thermostat halfway across the room reads 72°F. Floors are often cooler because cold air settles, and because they may be over an unheated basement or crawlspace. So even in the same room, your body can be in a pocket of cooler air while the thermostat is happy. ### 3. Different rooms can be wildly different Unless you have a perfectly balanced zoned system (and most homes don’t), bedrooms far from the thermostat, upstairs/downstairs splits, and rooms with lots of windows will be several degrees off. In winter, that upstairs might be warmer because heat rises, but a north-facing bedroom could be 5–8 degrees colder than where the thermostat sits in the hallway. ### 4. The system doesn’t actively “push” all air to 72°F A furnace or heat pump cycles on and off based on that one spot. It blows warm air for a while, then stops. Air temperatures constantly drift between cycles, and the mixing isn’t perfect. So you get warm peaks and cool troughs, especially in poorly insulated homes. Now, even if we magically eliminated all those variations and made the air temperature *truly* 72°F everywhere (like in a perfectly mixed climate chamber), you’d still feel colder in winter than in summer at the same 72°F air temperature—because of the **radiant heat loss** and **humidity** I mentioned earlier. Those aren’t just theories that depend on thermostat inaccuracy; they’re physiological facts. Your body radiates heat to cold surfaces and evaporative cooling increases in dry air, making you feel chillier regardless of what the thermostat says. So the thermostat reading is a target for one location, not a promise of uniform comfort. That’s precisely why 72°F in winter can still feel drafty and cold—your body is responding to the *actual* conditions around it, not just the number on the wall.
